WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
Carbon sits at the top of Group 4.

2
00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:04.680
Lead sits at the bottom.

3
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.360
Both atoms have four electrons
in their outer shell.

4
00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:10.160
Carbon uses all four.

5
00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.600
In carbon dioxide and carbon
tetrachloride, it is plus 4.

6
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.280
But in most of its compounds,
lead is plus 2.

7
00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:21.360
Two of its four electrons sit unused.

8
00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.900
Same group, same four electrons.

9
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:27.240
So why does lead usually
use only two of them?

10
00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:31.460
In this mission, you'll follow
Group 4 from non-metal to metal,

11
00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:34.840
comparing its oxides and
chlorides on the way down.

12
00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:36.380
Then you'll test for lead ions,

13
00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:41.560
and watch a bright yellow solid
re-form as shiny golden plates.

14
00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:45.710
Your mission: explain why
lead so often stops at two.